var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"models/#flow-models","page":"Flow models","text":"A flow model is an invertible map f = f_L circ dots circ f_1 of mathbbR^d onto itself, each layer f_l invertible with a Jacobian that is cheap to compute. It is built in two steps: FlowModel sizes the layers for the dimension d, and compile sets their parameters, explicit ones or drawn from a generator, giving the CompiledFlowModel the Flow node takes.\n\njulia> model = FlowModel(3, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(RadialFlow()), AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow())));\n\njulia> nr_params(model)   # two radial flows of 3 parameters, two planar flows of 3\n12\n\njulia> compiled = compile(model);\n\njulia> x = [0.1, -0.4, 0.7];\n\njulia> FlowMessagePassingRules.backward(compiled, FlowMessagePassingRules.forward(compiled, x)) ≈ x\ntrue","title":"Flow models"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Models","page":"Flow models","text":"","title":"Models"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Layers","page":"Flow models","text":"A model's tuple of layers may start with an InputLayer in place of the dimension. An AdditiveCouplingLayer holds the coupling flows and, by default, is followed by a random PermutationLayer, so that the next coupling layer mixes the coordinates in another order. Its partition_dim sets the size of the blocks it couples: one coordinate each by default, or two halves, partition_dim = d / 2, as in NICE.","title":"Layers"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Coupling-flows","page":"Flow models","text":"The functions an AdditiveCouplingLayer adds, one per coordinate after the first. Given without arguments they are placeholders, sized by the model and given parameters by compile.","title":"Coupling flows"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Evaluating-a-model","page":"Flow models","text":"The functions the rules call on a compiled model are public but not exported, and are called qualified, FlowMessagePassingRules.forward(model, x). Each broadcasts over a vector of points with the model fixed.","title":"Evaluating a model"},{"category":"section","location":"models/#Permutations","page":"Flow models","text":"","title":"Permutations"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.FlowModel","page":"Flow models","text":"FlowModel([rng,] dim::Int, layers::Tuple)\nFlowModel([rng,] layers::Tuple)\n\nA flow model of dimension dim, a tuple of layers whose sizes are set and whose parameters are not: compile gives it its parameters, and the Flow node takes the CompiledFlowModel that results.\n\nArguments\n\nrng: the generator the random permutations are drawn from, those of a PermutationLayer() and of an AdditiveCouplingLayer with permute = true. Default: the task's generator.\ndim: the dimension of the input, and of the output.\nlayers: the layers, applied in order: AdditiveCouplingLayers and PermutationLayers. In the second form the tuple starts with an InputLayer(dim), which gives the dimension.\n\nThrows\n\nAn AssertionError when the second form's tuple does not start with an InputLayer.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> model = FlowModel(2, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow()), AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow())));\n\njulia> length(getlayers(model))   # each coupling layer is followed by a permutation\n4\n\njulia> nr_params(model)\n6\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.FlowModel"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.CompiledFlowModel","page":"Flow models","text":"CompiledFlowModel\n\nA flow model with its parameters set, which the Flow node takes through FlowApproximation. It is made by compile from a FlowModel, never by hand, so that every layer and coupling flow is sized for the model's dimension.\n\nIts output and inverse are FlowMessagePassingRules.forward and FlowMessagePassingRules.backward, its Jacobians FlowMessagePassingRules.jacobian and FlowMessagePassingRules.inv_jacobian, with both at once from FlowMessagePassingRules.forward_jacobian and FlowMessagePassingRules.backward_inv_jacobian; getlayers gives its layers, nr_params the number of its parameters, and eltype(model) their element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.CompiledFlowModel"},{"category":"method","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.compile-Tuple{FlowModel}","page":"Flow models","text":"compile([rng,] model::FlowModel) -> CompiledFlowModel\n\nGive a flow model random parameters, drawn from rng, by default the task's generator. Each coupling flow draws its own, in the order of the layers: see PlanarFlow and RadialFlow for the distributions. Permutations are the model's, drawn when it was built.\n\nSee also compile(::FlowModel, ::Vector).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.compile"},{"category":"method","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.compile-Tuple{FlowModel, Vector}","page":"Flow models","text":"compile(model::FlowModel, params::Vector) -> CompiledFlowModel\n\nGive a flow model the parameters params, which the layers take in order, each coupling flow its own consecutive slice. The result is deterministic: nothing is drawn.\n\nThrows\n\nAn AssertionError when length(params) is not nr_params(model).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> model = compile(FlowModel(2, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow(); permute = false),)), [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n\njulia> FlowMessagePassingRules.forward(model, [1.0, 2.0]) ≈ [1.0, 2.0 + 1.0 + tanh(2.0 + 3.0)]\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.compile"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.nr_params","page":"Flow models","text":"nr_params(model)\n\nThe number of parameters of a flow model, of a layer or of a coupling flow, compiled or not: the length of the vector compile(model, params) takes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.nr_params"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.getlayers","page":"Flow models","text":"getlayers(model)\n\nThe tuple of layers of a FlowModel or of a CompiledFlowModel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.getlayers"},{"category":"method","location":"models/#Base.eltype-Tuple{CompiledFlowModel}","page":"Flow models","text":"eltype(model::CompiledFlowModel)\n\nThe element type of the compiled flow model's parameters, promoted over its layers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.eltype"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.InputLayer","page":"Flow models","text":"InputLayer(dim::Int)\n\nThe first element of a layer tuple given to FlowModel(layers), which gives the model's dimension dim; FlowModel((InputLayer(dim), layers...)) is FlowModel(dim, layers).\n\nThrows\n\nAn AssertionError unless dim > 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.InputLayer"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.AdditiveCouplingLayer","page":"Flow models","text":"AdditiveCouplingLayer(flow; partition_dim::Int = 1, permute::Bool = true)\n\nA layer of a FlowModel that adds to each coordinate a coupling flow of the one before it. For an input x in mathbbR^d,\n\ny_1 = x_1 qquad y_k = x_k + f_k-1(x_k-1) quad k = 2 dots d\n\nwith a flow f_k-1 of its own for each k, all of the kind of flow. Here x_k is a partition of partition_dim coordinates, one coordinate by default. It is invertible whatever the flows are, x_k = y_k - f_k-1(x_k-1) in order, and its Jacobian is block lower-triangular with identity blocks on the diagonal, so its determinant is one. With two partitions, partition_dim = d / 2, it is the layer of Dinh, Krueger and Bengio, \"NICE: Non-linear independent components estimation\", arXiv:1410.8516 (2014).\n\nArguments\n\nflow: the coupling flow placeholder, PlanarFlow() or RadialFlow(). The layer's dimension is set when it is wrapped in a FlowModel, and the flows' parameters by compile.\n\nKeywords\n\npartition_dim: the size of a partition, which must divide the model's dimension: a model of another dimension is an ArgumentError. Default 1, every coordinate its own partition.\npermute: whether the model places a random PermutationLayer after the layer, so that the next coupling layer mixes the coordinates in another order. Default true.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> model = compile(FlowModel(3, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow(); permute = false),)), zeros(6));\n\njulia> FlowMessagePassingRules.forward(model, [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]) ≈ [1.0, 3.0, 5.0]   # u = 0: each fₖ(x) = x\ntrue\n\nTwo halves of four coordinates, as in NICE, with one planar flow on two coordinates:\n\njulia> model = compile(FlowModel(4, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow(); partition_dim = 2, permute = false),)), zeros(5));\n\njulia> FlowMessagePassingRules.forward(model, [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]) ≈ [1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 6.0]   # y₂ = x₂ + x₁\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.AdditiveCouplingLayer"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.PermutationLayer","page":"Flow models","text":"PermutationLayer(dim::Int, P::PermutationMatrix)\nPermutationLayer(P::PermutationMatrix)\nPermutationLayer([rng,] dim::Integer)\nPermutationLayer()\n\nA layer of a FlowModel that permutes its input, y = P x for a PermutationMatrix P. It has no parameters, and its inverse is x = P^top y.\n\nPermutationLayer(P) takes the matrix, which must be square. PermutationLayer([rng,] dim) draws a random dim × dim one from rng, by default the task's generator. PermutationLayer() is a placeholder: its dimension and its random permutation are set when it is wrapped in a FlowModel, from the model's generator.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> layer = PermutationLayer(PermutationMatrix([2, 3, 1]));\n\njulia> FlowMessagePassingRules.forward(layer, [10.0, 20.0, 30.0])\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 20.0\n 30.0\n 10.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.PermutationLayer"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.PlanarFlow","page":"Flow models","text":"PlanarFlow(u, w, b)\nPlanarFlow([rng,] dim::Int)\nPlanarFlow()\n\nThe planar flow, a coupling flow for an AdditiveCouplingLayer:\n\nf(x) = x + u tanh(w^top x + b) qquad x u w in mathbbR^D  b in mathbbR\n\nIt contracts and expands the space along u. It has 2D + 1 parameters, taken by compile in the order u w b. It was introduced by Rezende and Mohamed, \"Variational inference with normalizing flows\", ICML 2015.\n\nPlanarFlow(u, w, b) takes the parameters, u and w both scalars or both vectors of the same length. PlanarFlow([rng,] dim) draws them from a standard normal, using rng, by default the task's generator: scalars for dim == 1, vectors of length dim otherwise. PlanarFlow() is the placeholder an AdditiveCouplingLayer takes: its dimension is set by the model, and its parameters by compile, which draws them the same way.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> FlowMessagePassingRules.forward(PlanarFlow(1.0, 2.0, 3.0), 1.0) ≈ 1.0 + tanh(5.0)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.PlanarFlow"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.RadialFlow","page":"Flow models","text":"RadialFlow(z0, α, β)\nRadialFlow([rng,] dim::Int)\nRadialFlow()\n\nThe radial flow, a coupling flow for an AdditiveCouplingLayer:\n\nf(x) = x + fracbeta (x - z_0)alpha + lVert x - z_0 rVert qquad x z_0 in mathbbR^D  alpha  0  beta in mathbbR\n\nIt contracts and expands the space around z_0. It has D + 2 parameters, taken by compile in the order z_0 alpha beta. It was introduced by Rezende and Mohamed, \"Variational inference with normalizing flows\", ICML 2015.\n\nRadialFlow(z0, α, β) takes the parameters, α > 0. RadialFlow([rng,] dim) draws them using rng, by default the task's generator: z0 and β from a standard normal, α uniformly from 0 1). RadialFlow() is the placeholder an AdditiveCouplingLayer takes: its dimension is set by the model, and its parameters by compile, which draws them the same way.\n\nThrows\n\nAn AssertionError unless α > 0.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> FlowMessagePassingRules.forward(RadialFlow(0.0, 1.0, 2.0), 1.0) ≈ 1.0 + 2.0 / 2.0\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.RadialFlow"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.forward","page":"Flow models","text":"forward(model::CompiledFlowModel, input::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nThe output of the compiled flow model at input, passing it through every layer in order. It has methods for the layers and the coupling flows too, and broadcasts over a vector of inputs with the model fixed, forward.(model, inputs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.forward"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.backward","page":"Flow models","text":"backward(model::CompiledFlowModel, output::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nThe inverse of FlowMessagePassingRules.forward: the input of the compiled flow model that gives output, passing it back through every layer in reverse order. It broadcasts over a vector of outputs with the model fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.backward"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.jacobian","page":"Flow models","text":"jacobian(model::CompiledFlowModel, input::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nThe Jacobian matrix of the compiled flow model at input, the product of its layers' Jacobians. It has methods for the layers and the coupling flows too, and broadcasts over a vector of inputs with the model fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.inv_jacobian","page":"Flow models","text":"inv_jacobian(model::CompiledFlowModel, output::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nThe Jacobian matrix of the inverse flow, FlowMessagePassingRules.backward, at output: the inverse of jacobian(model, backward(model, output)). It broadcasts over a vector of outputs with the model fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.inv_jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.forward_jacobian","page":"Flow models","text":"forward_jacobian(model::CompiledFlowModel, input::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nThe output and the Jacobian matrix of the compiled flow model at input in one pass, the tuple (forward(model, input), jacobian(model, input)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.forward_jacobian"},{"category":"function","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.backward_inv_jacobian","page":"Flow models","text":"backward_inv_jacobian(model::CompiledFlowModel, output::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nThe input and the inverse flow's Jacobian matrix at output in one pass, the tuple (backward(model, output), inv_jacobian(model, output)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.backward_inv_jacobian"},{"category":"type","location":"models/#FlowMessagePassingRules.PermutationMatrix","page":"Flow models","text":"PermutationMatrix(ind::Vector{<:Integer})\nPermutationMatrix([rng,] dim::Integer; switch_first::Bool = true)\n\nA permutation matrix, with ones at (k, ind[k]) for k = 1:length(ind) and zeros elsewhere, so that P * v is v[ind]. Its inverse is its adjoint, and products with vectors, square matrices and other permutations reorder entries without multiplying.\n\nArguments\n\nind: the permutation, a vector of the indices 1:n in some order.\nrng: the generator the random permutation is drawn from. Default: the task's generator.\ndim: the size of the random permutation, dim × dim.\n\nKeywords\n\nswitch_first: when true, a draw that keeps the first index in place swaps the first two entries, so the first element always moves. Default true.\n\nThrows\n\nA DimensionMismatch for a product with a matrix that is not square.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> PermutationMatrix([2, 1, 3]) * [10, 20, 30]\n3-element Vector{Int64}:\n 20\n 10\n 30\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.PermutationMatrix"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#flow-internals","page":"Internals","text":"The helpers the rules are built on. None is public; a contributor changing the rules reads them.\n\nThe two types the rules are declared under, one per method:\n\nThe linearisation rules for a message in precision form share these, one per direction:\n\nThe unscented rules push the sigma points through the model themselves:","title":"Internals"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#FlowMessagePassingRules.FlowLinearization","page":"Internals","text":"FlowLinearization\n\nThe type of a FlowApproximation whose method is Linearization, the algorithm the linearisation rules are declared under.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.FlowLinearization"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#FlowMessagePassingRules.FlowUnscented","page":"Internals","text":"FlowUnscented\n\nThe type of a FlowApproximation whose method is Unscented, the algorithm the unscented rules are declared under.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.FlowUnscented"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FlowMessagePassingRules.flow_forward_precision","page":"Internals","text":"flow_forward_precision(algo::FlowLinearization, m_in) -> MvNormalMeanPrecision\n\nThe linearised message towards out from a normal m_in in precision form, mean-precision or weighted-mean-precision alike: mean forward(model, μ) and precision Jᵢᵀ Λ Jᵢ, with Jᵢ the inverse flow's Jacobian at the output's mean forward(model, μ). It is the covariance form's message, inverted without inverting a matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.flow_forward_precision"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FlowMessagePassingRules.flow_backward_precision","page":"Internals","text":"flow_backward_precision(algo::FlowLinearization, m_out) -> MvNormalMeanPrecision\n\nThe linearised message towards in from a normal m_out in precision form: mean backward(model, μ) and precision Jᵀ Λ J, with J the flow's Jacobian at the input's mean backward(model, μ). It is the covariance form's message, inverted without inverting a matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.flow_backward_precision"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#FlowMessagePassingRules.flow_unscented_statistics","page":"Internals","text":"flow_unscented_statistics(f, model, method::Unscented, μ, Σ) -> MvNormalMeanCovariance\n\nThe normal of f(model, x) for x ~ N(μ, Σ) by the unscented transform, f being FlowMessagePassingRules.forward or FlowMessagePassingRules.backward. The sigma points are placed along the rows of the symmetric square root of (L + λ) Σ, not along a Cholesky factor as unscented_statistics places them: both are exact for an affine flow, and they differ for a nonlinear one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.flow_unscented_statistics"},{"category":"section","location":"#FlowMessagePassingRules","page":"Flow","text":"The Flow node of the ReactiveMP ecosystem: a deterministic, invertible transformation y = f(x) of a multivariate variable, with f a normalising-flow model built from layers. Use it to give a Gaussian latent variable a flexible, non-Gaussian image, or to learn a nonlinear map whose inverse and Jacobian are cheap. The flow models themselves, and their layers, live in this package too: see Flow models.\n\ninfo: Where these rules run\nReactiveMP runs these rules on a factor graph, which RxInfer builds from a GraphPPL model. The examples here call the rules directly, as a test does.\n\nThe site has three pages: this one, the node; Flow models, the models and their layers; and Internals, the helpers the rules are built on.","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Flow","text":"Flow is a deterministic node: it connects an input in and an output out of the same dimension through a compiled flow model. A message arriving on either side is pushed through the model, forwards through f or backwards through f^-1. It leaves as a multivariate normal, by linearisation with the model's own Jacobians or by the unscented transform. Both directions are exact for an affine flow and approximate otherwise.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Definition","page":"Flow","text":"p(y mid x) = deltabig(y - f(x)big) qquad x y in mathbbR^d\n\nwhere f = f_L circ dots circ f_1 is the composition of the model's layers, each invertible with a known Jacobian. The Dirac delta puts all the probability on y = f(x): the output is a function of the input, with no noise.","title":"Definition"},{"category":"section","location":"#Interfaces","page":"Flow","text":"using FlowMessagePassingRules, MessagePassingRulesBase, MessagePassingRulesApproximations, BayesBase, ExponentialFamily\nMessagePassingRulesBase.nodespec(Flow)\nNodeSpec: Flow (deterministic)\n  interfaces:        out, in\n  default algorithm: MessagePassingRulesBase.DefaultAlgorithm()\n  static inputs:     none\n  defined:           src/node.jl:118\n\nThe node has two interfaces.\n\nname aliases meaning messages the rules take\nout  the output y = f(x) multivariate normal\nin  the input x multivariate normal\n\nUnder Linearization the rules take MvNormalMeanCovariance, MvNormalMeanPrecision and MvNormalWeightedMeanPrecision, and return an MvNormalMeanCovariance for the first and an MvNormalMeanPrecision for the others; under Unscented they take any multivariate normal and return an MvNormalMeanCovariance. No rule reads a marginal.","title":"Interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"#Algorithm","page":"Flow","text":"The node's algorithm, FlowApproximation(model; method = Linearization()), carries the CompiledFlowModel and the method, Linearization() or Unscented(). A model must name it for every Flow node: the node has no default algorithm, so under DefaultAlgorithm no rule exists. The node declares no dependencies, so each rule takes the inputs of the default scheme: the message on the other interface.","title":"Algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"#Supported-rules","page":"Flow","text":"MessagePassingRulesBase.rule_coverage(Flow)\nRule coverage for Flow\n        │ DefaultAlgorithm │ FlowApproximation{<:AbstractCompiledFlowModel, <:Linearization} │ FlowApproximation{<:AbstractCompiledFlowModel, <:Unscented}\n  → out │                  │ ✓×3                                                             │ ✓                                                          \n  → in  │                  │ ✓×3                                                             │ ✓                                                          \n\nThe two FlowApproximation columns are its two methods. The first is linearisation, with three rules per edge, one per normal form; the second is the unscented transform. Every rule is a belief propagation message. There is no marginal rule, since the marginal of in is the product of its two messages. There is no average energy, since a deterministic node needs none: its contribution to the Bethe free energy is minus the entropy of q(in), as good as the approximation behind it.","title":"Supported rules"},{"category":"section","location":"#Example","page":"Flow","text":"A two-dimensional model of one coupling layer with a planar flow, given its three parameters, and the message towards out from a standard normal centred at [1, 2]:\n\nmodel = compile(FlowModel(2, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow(); permute = false),)), [1.0, 2.0, 3.0])\n\n@call_message_update_rule(\n    node = Flow, target = :out, algorithm = FlowApproximation(model),\n    m = (in = MvNormalMeanCovariance([1.0, 2.0], [1.0 0.0; 0.0 1.0]),),\n)\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mRuleResult\u001b[22m  message of Flow towards :out  \u001b[90m· messages\u001b[39m\n  \u001b[0m\u001b[1mFlow\u001b[22m\n    out  \u001b[32m\u001b[1m◀══  target\u001b[22m\u001b[39m  \u001b[32mMvNormalMeanCovariance( μ: [1.0, 3.99991] Σ: [1.0 1.00036; 1.00036 2.00073] )\u001b[39m\n    in   \u001b[36m──▶  m\u001b[39m  MvNormalMeanCovariance( μ: [1.0, 2.0] Σ: [1.0 0.0; 0.0 1.0] )\n  result     MvNormalMeanCovariance( μ: [1.0, 3.99991] Σ: [1.0 1.00036; 1.00036 2.00073] )\n  logscale   \u001b[33mundefined: the message rule for Flow towards :out under FlowApproximation{<:FlowMessagePassingRules.AbstractCompiledFlowModel, <:MessagePassingRulesApproximations.Linearization} declares no `logscale`\u001b[39m\n  algorithm  FlowApproximation{CompiledFlowModel{1, Tuple{AdditiveCouplingLayer{Tuple{PlanarFlow{Float64, Float64}}}}}, Linearizatio…\n  rule       m[:in]::ExponentialFamily.MvNormalMeanCovariance  @ src/rules.jl:24\n\u001b[90m  and 3 other rules for this target\u001b[39m\n\n@call_message_update_rule runs the rule and draws the message it read. Under linearisation the mean of the result is the image of the input's mean, FlowMessagePassingRules.forward of it. The unscented transform gives a mean close to it. getresult extracts both for the comparison:\n\njulia> model = compile(FlowModel(2, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow(); permute = false),)), [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n\njulia> m_in = MvNormalMeanCovariance([1.0, 2.0], [1.0 0.0; 0.0 1.0]);\n\njulia> linearised = getresult(@call_message_update_rule(node = Flow, target = :out, m = (in = m_in,), algorithm = FlowApproximation(model)));\n\njulia> mean(linearised) ≈ FlowMessagePassingRules.forward(model, [1.0, 2.0])\ntrue\n\njulia> unscented = getresult(@call_message_update_rule(node = Flow, target = :out, m = (in = m_in,), algorithm = FlowApproximation(model; method = Unscented())));\n\njulia> isapprox(mean(unscented), mean(linearised); atol = 1e-2)\ntrue","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Limitations","page":"Flow","text":"Multivariate normal messages only, in and out; no rule takes a marginal.\nNo average energy, as for every deterministic node, and a free energy only as good as the linearisation or the unscented transform behind the messages.\nThe model's parameters are fixed once compiled: the node does not learn them.\nUnder Linearization, the rules for a message in precision form evaluate the Jacobian at the message's mean read as a point on the other side of the flow, where the covariance form evaluates it at the mean itself: the two forms agree for a two-dimensional model of one coupling layer, and differ in general for a nonlinear flow.","title":"Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"Flow","text":"The node and its algorithm, and the algorithm's accessors, called qualified:","title":"API"},{"category":"module","location":"#FlowMessagePassingRules","page":"Flow","text":"FlowMessagePassingRules\n\nThe Flow node, the deterministic node y = f(x) for an invertible flow model f, and the flow models it is built from. A model is a tuple of layers, FlowModel, given its parameters by compile; the layers are InputLayer, AdditiveCouplingLayer and PermutationLayer, and a coupling layer holds a coupling flow, PlanarFlow or RadialFlow. PermutationMatrix is the permutation a PermutationLayer applies.\n\nThe node's rules push a multivariate normal through the compiled model, forwards towards out and backwards, through the inverse, towards in, by linearisation or by the unscented transform. They run under FlowApproximation, which carries the model, and which the model must give: under DefaultAlgorithm no rule exists.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> model = compile(FlowModel(2, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow(); permute = false),)), [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n\njulia> result = @call_message_update_rule(\n           node = Flow, target = :out, algorithm = FlowApproximation(model),\n           m = (in = MvNormalMeanCovariance([1.0, 2.0], [1.0 0.0; 0.0 1.0]),),\n       );\n\njulia> μ, Σ = mean_cov(getresult(result));\n\njulia> μ ≈ [1.0, 3.0 + tanh(5.0)]   # y₂ = x₂ + x₁ + tanh(2x₁ + 3) at the mean\ntrue\n\njulia> J = [1.0 0.0; 1 + 2 * (1 - tanh(5.0)^2) 1.0];   # the Jacobian at the mean\n\njulia> Σ ≈ J * J'\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules"},{"category":"type","location":"#FlowMessagePassingRules.Flow","page":"Flow","text":"Flow\n\nThe deterministic node y = f(x), for an invertible flow model f, a CompiledFlowModel of dimension d:\n\np(y mid x) = deltabig(y - f(x)big) qquad x y in mathbbR^d\n\nInterfaces\n\nout: the output y, multivariate;\nin: the input x, multivariate, of the model's dimension.\n\nAlgorithm\n\nThe rules run under FlowApproximation, which carries the model and the method, and which the model must give. The node declares no algorithm of its own, so under DefaultAlgorithm no rule exists and a call throws a RuleNotFoundError.\n\nRules\n\nBelief propagation only, one message rule per edge and per method, each taking a multivariate normal message on the other edge and returning a multivariate normal:\n\ntowards out, the message on in pushed forwards through f;\ntowards in, the message on out pulled back through f^-1.\n\nUnder Linearization the model's own Jacobian J gives mathcalN(f(mu) J Sigma J^top); the rules take MvNormalMeanCovariance, MvNormalMeanPrecision and MvNormalWeightedMeanPrecision, and return an MvNormalMeanCovariance for the first and an MvNormalMeanPrecision for the others. Under Unscented the sigma points give the moments; the rules take any multivariate normal and return an MvNormalMeanCovariance.\n\nThe node has no marginal rule, since the marginal of in is the product of its two messages, and no average energy, since a deterministic node needs none: its contribution to the Bethe free energy is minus the entropy of q(in), which is exact only as far as the approximation is.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> model = compile(FlowModel(2, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow(); permute = false),)), [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n\njulia> y = [1.0, 3.0 + tanh(5.0)];   # f([1.0, 2.0])\n\njulia> result = @call_message_update_rule(\n           node = Flow, target = :in, algorithm = FlowApproximation(model),\n           m = (out = MvNormalMeanPrecision(y, [1.0 0.0; 0.0 1.0]),),\n       );\n\njulia> mean(getresult(result)) ≈ [1.0, 2.0]\ntrue\n\nSee also FlowModel, compile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.Flow"},{"category":"type","location":"#FlowMessagePassingRules.FlowApproximation","page":"Flow","text":"FlowApproximation(model::CompiledFlowModel; method = Linearization())\nFlowApproximation(model::CompiledFlowModel, method)\n\nFlow's algorithm: the compiled flow model, and the method that pushes a normal through it. A model using the node must give it; the node has no default algorithm.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel: the compiled flow, a CompiledFlowModel from compile.\n\nKeywords\n\nmethod: Linearization(), which uses the model's own Jacobians, or Unscented, which uses sigma points. Unscented() takes its dimension from each input; Unscented(dim) fixes it, and a rule throws a DimensionMismatch when dim is not the model's dimension. Default Linearization().\n\nExamples\n\njulia> model = compile(FlowModel(2, (AdditiveCouplingLayer(PlanarFlow()),)), [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n\njulia> FlowApproximation(model).method isa Linearization\ntrue\n\njulia> FlowApproximation(model; method = Unscented(2)).method isa Unscented\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.FlowApproximation"},{"category":"function","location":"#FlowMessagePassingRules.getmodel","page":"Flow","text":"FlowMessagePassingRules.getmodel(algo::FlowApproximation) -> CompiledFlowModel\n\nThe compiled flow model of a FlowApproximation. Public, not exported: call it qualified, since RxInfer exports a getmodel of its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.getmodel"},{"category":"function","location":"#FlowMessagePassingRules.getmethod","page":"Flow","text":"FlowMessagePassingRules.getmethod(algo::FlowApproximation)\n\nThe method of a FlowApproximation, a Linearization or an Unscented. Public, not exported: call it qualified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlowMessagePassingRules.getmethod"}]
}
